</script><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Gaza Food Insecurity 2026: Latest IPC Assessment and Projections | World Food Crisis</title><meta name="description" content="The IPC's July 2026 Gaza assessment projected more than 1.4 million people in Crisis or worse through December. See the data, timeframe and caveats."><link rel="canonical" href="https://worldfoodcrisis.pages.dev/food-crisis-by-country/gaza/"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Gaza Food Insecurity 2026: Latest IPC Assessment and Projections"><meta property="og:description" content="The IPC's July 2026 Gaza assessment projected more than 1.4 million people in Crisis or worse through December. See the data, timeframe and caveats."><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css"><script is:inline src="/assets/main.js" defer></script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Gaza Food Insecurity 2026: Latest IPC Assessment and Projections","description":"The IPC's July 2026 Gaza assessment projected more than 1.4 million people in Crisis or worse through December. See the data, timeframe and caveats.","mainEntityOfPage":"https://worldfoodcrisis.pages.dev/food-crisis-by-country/gaza/","datePublished":"2026-10-15","dateModified":"2026-10-15","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"World Food Crisis Editorial Desk","url":"https://worldfoodcrisis.pages.dev/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"World Food Crisis","url":"https://worldfoodcrisis.pages.dev/"},"inLanguage":"en","articleSection":"Gaza food insecurity"}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How many people were projected to face acute food insecurity in Gaza in July–December 2026?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The July IPC assessment projected more than 1.4 million people, about 67% of the population, in Phase 3 or above for that period."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Did the IPC report describe any improvements?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. It attributed improvements in food security and nutrition after the October 2025 ceasefire to expanded humanitarian assistance, while stressing that conditions remained fragile."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a projection describe today's exact situation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A projection is tied to a period and information available when the report was prepared. Check for newer assessments before using it as a current count."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where is the original Gaza assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Read the IPC Gaza Strip analysis , released on 23 July 2026."}}]}</script></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><div class="topline"><div class="container topline-inner"><span>Evidence-led analysis</span><span>Facts before fear.</span></div></div><header class="site-header"><div class="container header-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><span class="brand-mark">F<span>+</span></span><span class="brand-words"><strong>World Food Crisis</strong><small>Facts before fear.</small></span></a><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="primary-nav"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span class="sr-only">Open navigation</span></button><nav class="primary-nav" id="primary-nav"><a href="/#analysis">Analysis</a><a href="/data/">Data desk</a><a href="/#guides">Practical guides</a><a href="/about/">About</a></nav></div></header><main id="main"><header class="article-hero"><div class="container"><nav class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/food-crisis-by-country/">Food crisis by country</a><span>›</span><span>Gaza Food Insecurity 2026: Latest IPC Assessment and Projections</span></nav><div class="article-hero-grid"><div><span class="article-category">Country briefing</span><h1 class="article-title">Gaza Food Insecurity 2026: Latest IPC Assessment and Projections</h1><p class="article-dek">The IPC's 23 July 2026 assessment reported that over 1.2 million people in Gaza faced Crisis or worse food insecurity during mid-April–June, and projected more than 1.4 million for July–December. These are period-specific assessment results and projections, not a live count.</p><div class="byline"><span><a href="/about/">World Food Crisis Editorial Desk</a></span><span>Updated 15 October 2026</span></div></div><aside class="article-hero-side"><span class="side-label">The key takeaway</span><strong>The July IPC report describes improvements after expanded assistance but says conditions remain fragile. Its July–December figure is a projection, and later developments may change conditions.</strong><p>Educational information, not an emergency alert.</p></aside></div></div></header><div class="container article-shell"><article class="article-content"><p class="lede">The Integrated Food Security Phase Classification (IPC) report released on 23 July 2026 found that more than 1.2 million people in the Gaza Strip—59% of the population—faced Crisis or worse acute food insecurity during mid-April to 30 June. It projected more than 1.4 million people, or 67%, in Phase 3 or above during July–December 2026. The projection is period-bound and may be superseded by later evidence.</p>
<div class="answer-box"><strong>Key results in the 23 July IPC analysis</strong><ul><li><strong>Reference period:</strong> 16 April–30 June 2026.</li><li><strong>Phase 3 or above in the reference period:</strong> over 1.2 million people (59%).</li><li><strong>Projection period:</strong> 1 July–31 December 2026.</li><li><strong>Projected Phase 3 or above:</strong> more than 1.4 million people (67%).</li><li><strong>Phase 4 (Emergency):</strong> 212,000 people in the reference period and projected to remain at that level in the cited projection.</li><li><strong>Report release:</strong> 23 July 2026.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What the IPC report says</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://www.ipcinfo.org/ipc-country-analysis/details-map/en/c/1165303/?iso3=PSE">IPC Gaza Strip analysis</a> describes improved conditions after large-scale assistance following the October 2025 ceasefire, while stressing that food security and nutrition remain highly fragile. In the reference period, over 1.2 million people faced IPC Phase 3 or above, including 212,000 in Emergency (Phase 4). The projection for July–December anticipated more than 1.4 million people in Phase 3 or above, with all five governorates classified in Crisis (Phase 3).</p>
<p>Those results should be read together: the report describes gains linked to aid and continued significant need. Improvement relative to an earlier period does not mean the situation is secure or that projected levels are a live measurement for any particular day. Conditions can change with access, conflict, prices, assistance coverage and the ability of households to earn a living.</p>
<h2>Why access and affordability matter as much as food entering Gaza</h2>
<p>Food security is not only about whether a shipment crosses a border. People must also be able to obtain food in markets or through assistance, and that food must be safe, appropriate and reliably available. The IPC report notes constraints including unpredictable access, high costs, concerns about the quality of imported commodities and low purchasing power. These factors can limit household access even when goods or assistance enter an area.</p>
<p>Infrastructure damage, displacement, livelihood loss and limited services can make ordinary food access extremely difficult. Prices can reflect costs and interruptions along the route, but the retail price alone cannot describe the scale of acute food insecurity. An assessment that measures food consumption, coping strategies and other outcomes adds information that a price index cannot supply.</p>
<h2>How to interpret IPC phases and projections</h2>
<p>IPC Phase 3, Crisis, indicates that households face significant food-consumption gaps or rely on damaging coping strategies. Phase 4, Emergency, indicates more severe outcomes and large food gaps. Phase 5 Catastrophe describes the most severe household-level phase. Famine is an area-level classification requiring separate evidence and criteria; the words should not be used interchangeably.</p>
<p>A projection is not a claim that every person in a phase experiences the same outcome. It summarizes estimates for a defined population, geography and period. The reported percentage is tied to the assessment's population denominator and methodology. Use the original IPC snapshot and report when quoting the numbers, particularly in a fast-changing context.</p>
<h2>Why conditions can improve and still remain fragile</h2>
<p>The IPC report attributes measurable improvements in food security and nutrition to expanded humanitarian assistance following the October 2025 ceasefire. It also says needs remain high and conditions are sensitive to changes in assistance and access. Where livelihoods have collapsed or families have used up their coping mechanisms, a reduction in aid, renewed hostilities, blocked commercial access or rising prices can quickly reverse gains.</p>
<p>This helps explain why reporting should avoid two extremes: using an older famine classification as if it necessarily describes the current period, or treating an improvement in indicators as proof that the crisis has ended. A dated assessment should be quoted for its own period, and any later changes should be checked against new primary evidence.</p>
<h2>What indicators should be monitored?</h2>
<ul><li><strong>New IPC analyses:</strong> release date, period covered, geographic units, phase definitions and projections.</li><li><strong>Humanitarian access:</strong> whether food, medical and nutrition supplies can enter, move and reach people safely.</li><li><strong>Market function and purchasing power:</strong> prices, availability of essential items, wages and household income.</li><li><strong>Assistance coverage:</strong> how many people receive assistance, what type is provided and whether funding is continuous.</li><li><strong>Nutrition and health:</strong> acute malnutrition, health services, clean water and sanitation conditions.</li><li><strong>Displacement and livelihoods:</strong> whether people can return safely, work, access markets and rebuild basic sources of income.</li></ul>
<p>No single measure fully describes the situation. Food entry totals do not prove every household can obtain food; a consumer price does not tell us how much aid arrived; and a projection must be read within the period and assumptions in the IPC report.</p>
<h2>How readers should use the latest available data</h2>
<p>For a fresh claim, start with the latest IPC portal update and check whether an assessment published after 23 July has changed the projection. Retain the report's language and date. If you cite “more than 1.4 million,” specify that this was the projection for July–December 2026 at the time of the cited analysis.</p>
<p>This page is an explainer, not an operational emergency report. For current humanitarian access, protection or assistance information, follow updates from established humanitarian agencies. The page does not offer safety guidance for travel or a real-time map of food availability.</p>
<h2>Sources and data notes</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://www.ipcinfo.org/ipc-country-analysis/details-map/en/c/1165303/?iso3=PSE">IPC: Gaza Strip acute food-insecurity analysis, released 23 July 2026</a>.</li><li><a href="https://www.ipcinfo.org/index.php?id=95442">IPC summary: assistance gains and fragile conditions through December 2026</a>.</li><li><a href="https://www.wfp.org/publications">World Food Programme publications</a> for subsequent operational reporting.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> the July IPC assessment documented substantial acute food insecurity and projected further severe need through December 2026, despite improvements associated with increased assistance. Keep the assessment and projection dates attached to the numbers, and check newer evidence before treating them as current.</p>
<section class="article-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><h2 id="faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How many people were projected to face acute food insecurity in Gaza in July–December 2026?</h3><p>The July IPC assessment projected more than 1.4 million people, about 67% of the population, in Phase 3 or above for that period.</p><h3>Did the IPC report describe any improvements?</h3><p>Yes. It attributed improvements in food security and nutrition after the October 2025 ceasefire to expanded humanitarian assistance, while stressing that conditions remained fragile.</p><h3>Does a projection describe today's exact situation?</h3><p>No. A projection is tied to a period and information available when the report was prepared. Check for newer assessments before using it as a current count.</p><h3>Where is the original Gaza assessment?</h3><p>Read the <a href="https://www.ipcinfo.org/ipc-country-analysis/details-map/en/c/1165303/?iso3=PSE">IPC Gaza Strip analysis</a>, released on 23 July 2026.</p></section></article><aside class="article-aside"><div class="aside-card"><h3>Useful resources</h3><a href="/data/">Food-supply data desk</a><a href="/sources/">Primary sources</a><a href="/methodology/">Editorial methodology</a><a href="/tools/food-budget-impact/">Budget calculator</a></div></aside></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="container footer-bottom"><span>© <span data-year>2026</span> World Food Crisis</span><span>Sources over speculation.</span></div></footer></body></html>